title: Creamy Dal
author: Stephanie
course: Side Dish
method: slow-cook
source: https://iamafoodblog.com/dishooms-black-dal-and-garlic-naan
cuisine: Indian
protein: legumes
servings: 6
cook time: 24 hours
prep time: 10 minutes
alternative_name: Dishoom’s Black Dal Recipe
ingredient_count: 18
---

Soak the @black lentils{1%cup} in cold water overnight. The next day rinse and drain well. Place the lentils in a large #pot and cover with cold water by 2 inches. Bring to a hard boil over high heat for ~{5-10%minutes}, skimming if needed. Reduce the heat and simmer, stirring occasionally, until the lentils are soft, but hold their shape, about ~{40-60%minutes}. If needed, add water to the #pot to prevent the lentils from drying out. Drain and set aside.

Heat the #oven to 325°F. In a small dry #pan, over low heat, lightly toast the @whole cloves{2}, @star anise{1}, @ground coriander{1%tsp}, @ground cumin{1%tsp}, @cayenne pepper{1/2%tsp}, @fennel seeds{1/2%tsp}, @garam masala{1/2%tsp}, and @ground cardamom{1/4%tsp}, stirring constantly, until very fragrant, about ~{30-45%seconds}. Make sure not to burn! Remove the spices from the #pan and add to a #spice grinder{} or #mortar and pestle{}. Crush/grind until fine. Set aside.

In a small, oven-safe #heavy-bottomed pot{}, heat up @butter{1%tbsp} and @neutral oil{1%tbsp} over medium heat. Add the @onion{1%small}(finely diced) and cook, stirring, until soft, about ~{5-8%minutes}. Add the @ginger{1%tbsp}(minced) and @garlic{1%tbsp}(minced) and cook, stirring, for ~{1-2%minutes}.

Stir in the spices, @cinnamon stick{1}, and @bay leaf{1}. Add the @tomato paste{5.5%oz} and 1 cup of water to bring everything to a thick, soup-like consistency. Add the drained lentils to the #pot, adding a bit of water to thin out, if needed.

Tightly cover the #pot with foil (or the oven safe lid) and place in the #oven for ~{3-4%hours}, checking every ~{30%minutes} or so to stir. Add water, a tablespoon at a time, if needed, to maintain a soup-like consistency.

After 4 hours, take a note of the time and turn the #oven to 200°F. The next morning, when you get up, turn the temperature up to 300°F, check if you need to add a tiny bit of water, stir, then continue to cook in the #oven, covered, until you hit 24 hours from the time you started the dal in the #oven.

Remove the #pot from the #oven and stir in @heavy cream{2%tbsp} and @butter{2%tbsp}. Taste and season with @salt{to taste}. Enjoy with basmati rice or naan.
